=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — that Laptop2 and Laptop3 cannot communicate across switch1 — does not exist in the live lab. Direct probing shows the Layer 2 and Layer 3 path between the two devices is fully operational in both directions.

Both laptops are connected to switch1 (Laptop2 on eth3, Laptop3 on eth4) and both ports are correctly assigned to the same Linux bridge domain, br_vlan10. No ebtables or iptables rules exist on switch1; all chains are in default-ACCEPT policy with zero entries. The bridge FDB has both MACs learned correctly on their respective ports. Each laptop holds a fresh ARP entry for the other. Bidirectional pings (4 packets each way) complete with 0% loss and sub-millisecond RTTs. The user's assertion that "Laptop3's port has died or the switch has stopped passing traffic between our two desks" is factually incorrect.

The only structural observation is that Laptop0 sits in a separate bridge domain (br_vlan20, subnet 172.16.20.0/24) on switch1.eth7, but this is by design and is entirely unrelated to Laptop2/Laptop3 communication.

Root cause: There is no network fault. The L2 segment shared by Laptop2 and Laptop3 is intact and forwarding normally.

Corrective action: No network change is required. The helpdesk should redirect the user to investigate the file-sharing application itself (service stopped, firewall on the host OS, shared-folder permissions, etc.) rather than the network infrastructure.
=== END ===
